/*!
    \page qtbridges-python-index.html
    \title Qt Bridges - Python
    \brief How to get started with Qt Bridges using Python.

    Qt Bridges is a framework that simplifies the integration of Python with QtQuick/QML.
    It connects Python backends to QML frontends, improving development efficiency.
    The framework offers an easy, declarative method for linking Python classes,
    properties, signals, and slots to QML, with minimal Qt knowledge required from
    the Python developer.

    \section1 Getting started

    \section2 Why Qt Bridges?

    As a Python developer, you can expose your Python classes to QML without
    learning the full PySide6 model/view system. QtBridge automatically handles
    property binding, signal connections, and model registration.

    \list
        \li Less boilerplate - No manual \c {@Slot}, \c {@Property}, or signal management required
        \li Automatic discovery - Public methods become callable from QML automatically
        \li Model integration - Python classes become QML-ready models with simple decorators
        \li Cross-language - Reuse the same QML files across multiple languages supported by Qt Bridges
    \endlist

    \section2 Setting up development environment

    Requirements:
    \list
        \li Python: Minimum version 3.9
    \endlist

    Build requirements:
    \list
        \li CMake: Minimum version 3.18
        \li Qt: Minimum version 6.10
        \li PySide6 and Shiboken6: Install version \c {6.10} before building with \c {pip install PySide6==6.10}
        \li Build system: \l {https://github.com/scikit-build/scikit-build-core} {scikit-build-core} is used
           for building and packaging the project
        \li Documentation QDoc (Qt's built-in tool for generating API reference documentation)
        \li Testing: pytest framework with pytest-qt plugin
    \endlist

    Dependencies:
    QtBridge automatically installs the required runtime dependencies for Python:
        \li NumPy
    \list
        \li PySide6
        \li Shiboken6
    \endlist
    You don’t need to manually install these; they are pulled in when you install QtBridge.

    \section2 Installation

    \code
        pip install qtbridge
    \endcode

    \section2 Building From source

    \e Build and Install the project

    \code
        pip install . -Ccmake.define.QT_PATHS=/path/to/qtpaths
    \endcode

    Building wheels:
    \code
        pip wheel . -Ccmake.define.QT_PATHS=/path/to/qtpaths
    \endcode

    \e Optional Build Flags
     Non-limited-api:

    \code
        pip wheel . -Ccmake.define.QT_PATHS=/path/to/qtpaths \
                    -Cwheel.py-api=py3
    \endcode

    Debug build:
    \code
    pip wheel . -Ccmake.define.QT_PATHS=/path/to/qtpaths \
                -Ccmake.define.CMAKE_INSTALL_DO_STRIP=0 \
                -Ccmake.define.CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
    \endcode

    \e Install Development Dependencies
    Required for building documentation and running tests:

    \code
    pip install -e ".[dev]"
    \endcode

    \e Build Documentation with QDoc
    After installing development dependencies, run:

    \code
    qdoc -project docs/QtBridges.qdoc
    qdoc -outputdir docs/html
    \endcode

    \e Running Tests

    To run all tests:
    \code
        pytest .
    \endcode

    To run a specific test class or method (for example, only the TestMethodReturn class):
    \code
        pytest tests/test_method_return.py::TestMethodReturn
    \endcode

    To run a specific test with debug logging enabled (shows QtBridges debug output):
    \code
        QT_LOGGING_RULES="qtbridges.debug=true" pytest tests/test_method_return.py::TestMethodReturn
    \endcode

    \section2 Running

    For running any project, go inside your directory and run the main file:

    \code
    python main.py
    \endcode

    \section1 Tutorials


    \section2 Developer Jokes Example

    This tutorial shows how to build a tiny QML app that displays random programming jokes. The
    backend is written in Python, and the UI is written in QML and QtBridge is used to expose Python
    objects to QML.

    \section3 What to Build

    A small QML app that:

    \list
        \li Picks a random joke question
        \li Shows the answer when the button is pressed
        \li Loads a new joke when the button is pressed again
        \li Uses neon colors for the answers
        \li Gets all joke data from Python
    \endlist

    Here is a preview of the UI logic:

    \list 1
        \li On startup → show a random joke question
        \li Press See Answer → show its answer with a random neon color
        \li Press Another One → show a new question
    \endlist

    \section3 1. Writing the Python Backend

    Create a file called \b {main.py}.

    First create the \c {ClassModel} for storing the list of list for questions and answers as well as
    the simple accessor  \c {getItem(row, column)}.

    \code
    from QtBridge import bridge_instance, qtbridge

    class QuestionModel:
        def __init__(self):
            # Add number of jokes
            self._questions = [
                ["Why do programmers prefer dark mode?",
                 "Because light attracts bugs."],
                ["Why do Java developers wear glasses?",
                 "Because they can't C#."],
                ["Why did the programmer quit his job?",
                 "He didn't get arrays."],
                ...
            ]
        # Method for accessing the data at a specific index
        def getItem(self, row: int, column: int) -> str:
            """Get either the question (0) or answer (1) for a row."""
            return self._questions[row][column]

        @property
        def items(self) -> dict[str]:
            """Optional: expose full dataset."""
            return self._questions

        def data(self) -> dict[str]:
            """Required by QtBridge."""
            return self._questions
    \endcode

    Expose the model as \c {QA_model} using \c {bridge_instance()} and register the \c {QuizModel} module.

    \code
    @qtbridge(module="QuizModel")
    def main():
        qa_model = QuestionModel()
        # Expose Python data to QML under the name: QA_model
        bridge_instance(qa_model, name="QA_model")

    if __name__ == "__main__":
        main()
    \endcode

    \section3 2. Writing the QML Frontend

    Create a file such as QuizModel.qml and make sure your project imports the Python module. This os
    the default uri for QtBridge.

    \code
    import backend 1.0
    \endcode

    Full QML Code

    \code
    import QtQuick
    import QtQuick.Controls
    import QtQuick.Layouts
    import backend 1.0

    ApplicationWindow {
        visible: true
        width: 440
        height: 200
        title: "Dev Jokes"

        // Neon accent colors for answers
        property var accentColors: ["#FF6F61", "#64FFDA", "#82B1FF", "#FFAB40", "#EA80FC"]

        // Random index
        property int currentIndex: Math.floor(Math.random() * QA_model.rowCount())

        // Boolean for the answer
        property bool showingAnswer: false

        // Simple background layer
        Rectangle {
            anchors.fill: parent
            color: "#1E1E1E"
        }
        // Layout for a text area and button
        ColumnLayout {
            anchors.centerIn: parent
            spacing: 20

            // An area for the questions and answers
            Rectangle {
                id: card
                color: "#1E1E1E"
                Layout.fillWidth: true

                Text {
                    id: displayText
                    text: QA_model.getItem(currentIndex, 0)  // show question
                    anchors.centerIn: parent
                    color: "#E0E0E0"
                }
            }

            // Button to show the answer and the next question
            Button {
                id: actionButton
                text: showingAnswer ? "Another One" : "See Answer"

                contentItem: Text {
                    text: actionButton.text
                    color: "#E0E0E0"
                }

                onClicked: {
                    if (!showingAnswer) {
                        // Show answer in random neon color
                        let idx = Math.floor(Math.random() * accentColors.length)
                        displayText.color = accentColors[idx]
                        displayText.text = QA_model.getItem(currentIndex, 1)
                        showingAnswer = true
                    } else {
                        // Load new random question
                        displayText.color = "#E0E0E0"
                        currentIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * QA_model.rowCount())
                        displayText.text = QA_model.getItem(currentIndex, 0)
                        showingAnswer = false
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    \endcode

    \section3 3. How the App works

    Loading Python data into QML: \c {bridge_instance()} exposes Python class instance to QML.

    \code
    bridge_instance(qa_model, name="QA_model")
    \endcode

    This gives a global QML object called \c {QA_model}.

    Getting the number of jokes: QtBridge automatically exposes a \c {rowCount()} function.

    \code
    QA_model.rowCount()
    \endcode

    Getting a question or answer: The custom Python method can be used for that.

    \code
    QA_model.getItem(row, column)
    \endcode

    Column index 0 is for question and column index 1 is for answer.

    Choosing a random joke: This will be initially shown on the window.

    \code
    property int currentIndex: Math.floor(Math.random() * QA_model.rowCount())
    \endcode

    Switching between question and answer: QML updates the displayed text dynamically.

    \code
    displayText.text = QA_model.getItem(currentIndex, 0)   // question
    displayText.text = QA_model.getItem(currentIndex, 1)   // answer
    \endcode

    \section3 Running the example

    \code
    python examples/quiz_example/main.py
    \endcode

    \section2 The Example minimal_app

    The \c {minimal_app} is a simple application that demonstrates the basics of
    Qt Bridges for Python. It shows how to expose a simple Python list model to
    QML and perform basic operations such as \c {insert}, \c {delete}, \c {move}, and \c {edit} items.

    The application is located in \c {examples/minimal_app} folder and can be run
    after all the prerequisites are installed.

    \section3 Running the example

    \code
    python examples/minimal_app/main.py
    \endcode
*/

